WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.930
Cargo feature flags are powerful because
they let crates enable optional behavior.

2
00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.150
A feature may add a dependency, activate
protocol support, switch a backend,

3
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.080
enable serialization formats, include
command-line integrations,

4
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.333
change runtime behavior, or alter platform
support.

5
00:00:12.413 --> 00:00:13.573
That flexibility is useful,

6
00:00:13.653 --> 00:00:16.068
but it also means features are part of the
security profile.

7
00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:17.694
Default features deserve attention.

8
00:00:17.774 --> 00:00:21.252
A crate may enable defaults that are
helpful for broad compatibility but unnecessary

9
00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:22.298
for a specific project.

10
00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:24.214
If a service only needs one small
capability,

11
00:00:24.294 --> 00:00:27.047
broad defaults may bring extra code paths,
dependencies, formats,

12
00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:28.431
or integrations into the build.

13
00:00:28.511 --> 00:00:31.796
Disabling unnecessary defaults can make
the artifact easier to review when it is

14
00:00:31.876 --> 00:00:32.987
practical and well tested.

15
00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:36.304
Feature interactions can surprise teams in
workspaces and dependency graphs.

16
00:00:36.384 --> 00:00:39.523
Cargo feature unification means a feature
enabled by one part of the graph can

17
00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:41.681
affect how a shared crate is built for
another part.

18
00:00:41.761 --> 00:00:44.707
Reviewers do not need to memorize every
internal detail to be effective,

19
00:00:44.787 --> 00:00:48.072
but they should know that workspace
feature choices can have wider effects than a

20
00:00:48.152 --> 00:00:49.553
single Cargo.toml line suggests.

21
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:51.903
Some feature choices are directly
security-relevant.

22
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:55.171
Features can influence parsing behavior,
cryptographic backend selection,

23
00:00:55.251 --> 00:00:57.763
transport support, native integrations,
logging behavior,

24
00:00:57.843 --> 00:00:59.292
or optional runtime capabilities.

25
00:00:59.372 --> 00:01:02.609
During dependency updates, a changed
feature set may matter as much as a changed

26
00:01:02.689 --> 00:01:03.365
version number.

27
00:01:03.445 --> 00:01:06.005
Defensive Cargo use means selecting
features intentionally.

28
00:01:06.085 --> 00:01:07.969
Document why important features are
enabled,

29
00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:10.126
disable unnecessary defaults where
appropriate,

30
00:01:10.206 --> 00:01:11.897
review feature changes in pull requests,

31
00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:15.165
and keep release tests aligned with the
feature combinations that production

32
00:01:15.245 --> 00:01:15.873
actually uses.

33
00:01:15.953 --> 00:01:18.900
Minimal behavior is easier to reason about
than accidental capability.
